#: ../guide/faq.md:1 e9737771aa2544d398f48aa83041dd9b
msgid "FAQ"
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:3 c457e7bdb9e54bf3928e4b80aca42f72
msgid ""
"Common questions and pitfalls. If you hit something not covered here, "
"[open an issue](https://github.com/gguan/qtrade/issues)."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:6 5936fbf9ac214bd3b7e9c309ac242906
msgid "Backtesting"
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:8 f86c04540f3948f1a340efc595d7b3cd
msgid "Why does my strategy underperform Buy & Hold?"
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:10 e6fbfa44f0954c02b97ac7653d126b7d
msgid ""
"Most simple strategies do. Buy & Hold extracts the asset's underlying "
"trend with zero commission and zero timing risk. To beat it your strategy"
" needs to either:"
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:14 07d3074d1ca94ab6a64211cdd25a14e4
msgid "**Avoid drawdowns** — sit out the worst periods (timing)."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:15 bd6d73130dac4cc2b52c39d3f790c2ad
msgid ""
"**Add diversification** — multi-asset reduces variance even with B&H-like"
" returns per asset."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:17 503c0eae7baf48b2bdc71c9a1631e4cc
msgid "**Use leverage** — explicit via `margin_ratio` or position sizing."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:18 88d06cf4c3784a279677c172049cdaa7
msgid "**Trade with edge** — short-side gains, mean reversion, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:20 256ba0dba3ec4ee28ed6beb06bec5ea5
msgid ""
"If your strategy has a positive Sharpe but loses to B&H, it might still "
"be valuable as a portfolio component (uncorrelated with passive long)."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:23 fbc77316a36b4f6f848aa19508e82ef6
msgid "How do I avoid look-ahead bias?"
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:25 483a0b5854b74b6da0bd64f0fc0ce485
msgid ""
"QTrade prevents the most common form by construction: `self.data` (and "
"`self.data_by_asset[asset]`) is sliced to `[:current_time]`, so you "
"*cannot* read future bars from inside `on_bar_close`."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:29 20d2620bbc51425ebdf8acb76aa2f54a
msgid "Where look-ahead can still creep in:"
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:31 3df6508a902b4fb5b28afcfb0b249b2b
msgid ""
"**In `prepare()`**, you have access to the full DataFrame. If you compute"
" an indicator that uses future information (e.g. "
"`df['Close'].shift(-1)`), `on_bar_close` will read it."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:34 cb71e89dc31945e4a7896625be10dc54
msgid ""
"**Limit / stop fills** assume the price actually traded at your level "
"inside the bar. For thinly-traded assets this overstates fill "
"probability."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:37 dc51bf7343c94806a791e779a435b699
msgid ""
"**`trade_on_close=True`** lets a market order fill at the current bar's "
"close — the same bar your signal was computed from. For some signals this"
" is realistic (close-of-bar signals), for others it's effectively "
"cheating. If unsure, set `trade_on_close=False` and fills happen at the "
"next bar's open."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:43 f8602971ca60401d9c7c56ed5b7c4dd0
msgid "What's the difference between `trade_on_close=True` and `False`?"
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md 5e47d6ed7eff4b9e91115e66f32e923d
msgid "Setting"
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md c6fecacc52a646f39f6052e5204c43ee
msgid "Market order placed in `on_bar_close()` for bar N fills at..."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md 880326f52bd34817b95b8047f0de5b88
msgid "`True`"
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md 4716c82f201744dbbad4555ce272611d
msgid "Bar N's **close** (same bar)"
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md 175eac5be9244634a4ee46d6d9b913ae
msgid "`False`"
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md 44ae38d9757149a78e003c396b126309
msgid "Bar N+1's **open**"
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:50 ee0d2ee4481b405ea6d333b3dfa5e05f
msgid ""
"`True` is convenient for backtests where your signal genuinely arrives at"
" end-of-bar (e.g. you compute on close prices). `False` is the realistic "
"setting if your signal could in practice take a tick to act on — slightly"
" conservative, recommended unless you have a reason."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:55 601850194de04008a741135266775796
msgid "My strategy fires no trades. Why?"
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:57 7414211f8a074685b340529bf222e362
msgid "A few common causes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:59 df9070c34be343cf84bb1dcb04a14a9a
msgid ""
"**Indicators are NaN at the start** — the first `window_size` bars are "
"warmup. `Backtest._start_idx` skips them automatically; if your data is "
"shorter than the warmup, you get zero trades."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:62 aaf7ec6305c44c0182031c02819b237c
msgid ""
"**`available_margin` is 0** — `margin_ratio < 1` lets you take leveraged "
"positions, but `available_margin` shrinks fast as you stack trades. Lower"
" the size or raise the ratio."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:65 829e68516c1546bca4f928fac30d1b96
msgid ""
"**`size=0` quietly does nothing** — `Order(size=0)` raises now (good!), "
"but if you compute `size = available_margin // close` with too-small "
"cash, default `Strategy.buy()` resolves to size 0 and Order construction "
"errors."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:70 2212e401032b4b81a53bc9251e265e4c
msgid "Why does my SL/TP fire at a price different from what I set?"
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:72 2ae5e522769b4a28bfadf77de1f39727
msgid ""
"The SL/TP triggers when the bar's `low` (for long stops) or `high` (for "
"long take-profits) crosses your level. The synthetic exit is recorded *at"
" your SL/TP level*, not at the bar's close. So if you set sl=99 and the "
"bar dips to low=98, your fill is recorded at 99, even though the bar "
"closed at 102."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:78 d867799c28534f818bd8a371c9eee567
msgid ""
"Caveat: ordinary stop *orders* (placed via `Order(stop=...)`) currently "
"fill at the bar's close, not the trigger price — a known fidelity gap "
"tracked as a future fix. SL/TP attached to a Trade work as described."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:82 3ff5486eaba248b1bcf7080d99311cb0
msgid "Optimization"
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:84 fb7904cecd4a4e6695abb77972febdf9
msgid "Why does my strategy look great in `optimize()` but bad live?"
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:86 2d739b4cc60047bfb74810e8ab078bc9
msgid ""
"In-sample overfitting. `optimize()` picks the params that look best on "
"the same data they're tested on — which is a textbook recipe for fitting "
"noise."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:90 01fed5c983c44075a9609d6fea339b43
msgid ""
"Use `walk_forward_optimize` instead — see [Walk-forward "
"optimization](walk_forward.md). Each parameter set is evaluated only on "
"data the strategy was never tuned on."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:94 791c9f4dd38d42f7aeb6ac504313230c
msgid ""
"If walk-forward Sharpe is much lower than `optimize` Sharpe, that gap is "
"your overfit. Common; expected; treat the walk-forward number as the "
"realistic one."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:98 e1328d90e8b64bbe895d39b87cff0f18
msgid "How do I pick the `maximize` metric?"
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:100 ed03b0a0a8b94893999d0f60ecd44521
msgid ""
"**Total Return [%]**: simple, but punishes nothing for risk. Strategies "
"with huge drawdowns can win."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:102 c32ce36fece643bd82ed8aeb629bcf5e
msgid ""
"**Sharpe Ratio**: most common default. Good for normal-ish return "
"distributions."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:104 fe4bc1eeeeed4fb4b3ffc035f39291ce
msgid ""
"**Sortino Ratio**: better for asymmetric / fat-tailed returns (trend-"
"followers)."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:106 555c343a8d4b49898ca5a0249b61e01e
msgid ""
"**Calmar Ratio**: rewards smooth equity curves; good if you care about "
"drawdown experience."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:109 d2210eeaa7f246358331803061359bad
msgid "Start with Sharpe unless you have a specific reason."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:111 2ed41e956c934effa8ed53abebfcfe34
msgid "My grid search is too slow."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:113 39579cfb58574b2290bf5e7558be497a
msgid "Two paths:"
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:115 83c4ebcda9f2431193945c8124c3ede1
msgid ""
"**Reduce grid size** — many strategies have one or two truly important "
"parameters; ranges of 4–6 values per param are usually enough. Don't "
"sweep everything at fine resolution."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:118 d5c73270640348ad8b7aa732e39f847a
msgid ""
"**Use vectorbt instead** — for thousands of param combinations, QTrade's "
"event loop is the wrong tool. See "
"[COMPARISON.md](https://github.com/gguan/qtrade/blob/main/COMPARISON.md)."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:122 636db1af43aa44bf9bd5d0e2877a293c
msgid "Multi-asset"
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:124 890eebf37997461588508eb23724b2a9
msgid "How do I align data from different sources?"
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:126 2cf0b254d3384e539d0cc3ef8c0ffd77
msgid ""
"QTrade requires every asset's DataFrame to share an identical "
"`DatetimeIndex`. Use a pandas join on the indexes before passing in:"
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:134 5e106dadd5a446b993bd06033ff85581
msgid ""
"For data with gaps (corporate actions, illiquid days) you might prefer "
"union + forward-fill — but **be careful**: forward-filled bars give the "
"impression of liquidity that wasn't there."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:138 0f09835851f94c5b81524ed9136bb7ee
msgid "Can I have different commission per asset?"
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:140 524823c3c6584c81a2f7928ca98e5435
msgid ""
"Not currently. `Commission` is a single instance shared across the "
"broker. To approximate per-asset cost, wrap your strategy logic to add "
"asset-specific slippage manually before placing orders."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:144 8446440ef85d4d6da8dcfd96612aeb59
msgid "Why does `self.position` raise in multi-asset mode?"
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:146 37d98d23eed14459a9261761c2403e3b
msgid ""
"Because there's no single Position to return — you have one per asset. "
"Use `self.positions[\"AAPL\"]` (dict access) instead. Same for "
"`self.data` → `self.data_by_asset[\"AAPL\"]`."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:150 fd46ee41728746a2aa6c266fac66ca07
msgid "Reinforcement Learning"
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:152 4100c1e2fffe482ebf30eece2ca13136
msgid "My agent learns to do nothing (reward = 0)."
msgstr ""

#: ../guide/faq.md:154 113ed0de9b4b4d6b9f3dd9ed1d62419b
msgid ""
"The default `RewardScheme` only rewards realized PnL on bars where a "
"trade closed. If your agent never closes trades, reward stays 0 and the "
"gradient signal is too sparse."
msgstr ""

